@nzakas @kaicataldo By the way, it seems the team can get code point length for a string in Node 4. The spread operator (`[...str].length`) or unicode RegExp (`str.split(/[^]{0}/u).length`) are not supported in Node.js 4, but I've just checked this way on Node.js 4.4.7 and it is OK:

``` js
const str = '\ud83d\udc0e\ud83d\udc71\u2764';

const strLength = str.length;

let strUniLength = 0;
for (let codePoint of str) strUniLength++;

console.log(`strLength: ${strLength}, strUniLength: ${strUniLength}`);
```

```
strLength: 5, strUniLength: 3
```
